1
00:00:07,933 --> 00:00:10,299
[engine starts]

2
00:00:10,799 --> 00:00:13,466
[engine revving]

3
00:00:17,466 --> 00:00:21,900
[Jerry] This is a 1968 Firebird 400
in solar red.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:28,233
The 400 was the most powerful Firebird
you could buy in 1968.

5
00:00:28,866 --> 00:00:30,500
[tires screeching]

6
00:00:31,799 --> 00:00:32,900
Picture yourself.

7
00:00:32,966 --> 00:00:35,301
You're a kid living
in Syosset, Long Island.

8
00:00:35,400 --> 00:00:37,000
You're a comedy nerd.

9
00:00:37,066 --> 00:00:38,733
Your life is a total zero.

10
00:00:39,766 --> 00:00:42,033
You sit home every day
watching Mike Douglas,

11
00:00:42,100 --> 00:00:44,600
Merv Griffin, Johnny Carson,
and David Letterman.

12
00:00:46,166 --> 00:00:48,466
What car could possibly break you out

13
00:00:48,533 --> 00:00:50,133
of your miserable loserness?

14
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:53,233
This is that car.

15
00:00:53,300 --> 00:00:55,966
Because it's got rally wheels,
hood-mounted tach,

16
00:00:56,033 --> 00:00:58,833
auxiliary gauge clusters,
sport wood steering wheel,

17
00:00:58,900 --> 00:01:03,099
rear mount antenna,
and custom exhaust tips.

18
00:01:03,966 --> 00:01:07,399
But at over $3,000, it's not something
you'd ever be able to afford.

19
00:01:07,466 --> 00:01:09,600
Your parents aren't 
going to buy it for you.

20
00:01:09,666 --> 00:01:12,066
It's not even that good 
of a car to begin with.

21
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:15,000
But you live on Long Island,
you don't know any better.

22
00:01:15,433 --> 00:01:17,566
This is as high as you could dream.

23
00:01:18,833 --> 00:01:21,933
The boy that I'm talking about
is my very special guest this week,

24
00:01:22,000 --> 00:01:23,166
Mr. Judd Apatow,

25
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:27,066
the very talented comedian,
filmmaker, and comedy super-geek.

26
00:01:28,033 --> 00:01:29,533
If he had a car like this,

27
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,399
we would never have all this
fantastic entertainment that he has made.

28
00:01:34,694 --> 00:01:37,664
So, today, I'm going to give him a chance
to see what it might have been like

29
00:01:38,199 --> 00:01:40,566
if he would've had this car at that time,

30
00:01:40,633 --> 00:01:44,100
and could have made his escape
from Syosset.

31
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:46,133
[phone ringing]

32
00:01:46,199 --> 00:01:47,600
-[Judd] Yes, sir?
-[Jerry] Judd?

33
00:01:47,666 --> 00:01:51,166
Nobody really knows
if it's Apatoe or Apatow.

34
00:01:51,233 --> 00:01:53,133
[Judd] It's Apatow, 
but if people say Apatoe,

35
00:01:53,179 --> 00:01:56,249
-I don't mind, 'cause it sounds fancier.
-[Jerry] Sounds like a dance step.

36
00:01:56,699 --> 00:01:59,666
[Judd] I wasn't sure
if coffee implied hamburger or not.

37
00:01:59,766 --> 00:02:01,766
-And a chocolate shake.
-[Jerry] Sure.

38
00:02:01,955 --> 00:02:05,024
Chocolate shake and a burger 
for breakfast, I love that idea.

39
00:02:05,091 --> 00:02:06,192
[Judd chuckles] Let's do it.

40
00:02:06,866 --> 00:02:11,966
I'm Jerry Seinfeld, and this is
Comedians in Cars Getting Coffee.

41
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:18,366
Hello.

42
00:02:18,605 --> 00:02:19,839
[Jerry] Wow, look at this.

43
00:02:19,933 --> 00:02:21,766
-So much stuff.
-[Judd] There's stuff, yeah.

44
00:02:21,833 --> 00:02:23,433
I like to see everything.

45
00:02:23,500 --> 00:02:26,500
[upbeat jazz music playing]

46
00:02:26,546 --> 00:02:29,215
-[Jerry] Is this where you write?
-Yes, this is where I write.

47
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:31,466
I try to do it in the morning
before it gets too busy.

48
00:02:31,684 --> 00:02:32,652
That is hilarious.

49
00:02:32,919 --> 00:02:36,156
[Judd] That is 1989.
I was doing stand-up then.

50
00:02:36,233 --> 00:02:38,266
And then I found
a picture of us with Garry...

51
00:02:38,491 --> 00:02:40,994
-[Jerry] Oh, my God.
-[Judd] ...at The Larry Sanders Show.

52
00:02:41,127 --> 00:02:42,962
Then here's the other thing
I was gonna show you.

53
00:02:43,033 --> 00:02:44,766
On your episode, you said to Garry,

54
00:02:44,833 --> 00:02:46,766
"What becomes of
all of David Brenner's jokes?"

55
00:02:46,833 --> 00:02:47,900
Material, yeah.

56
00:02:47,966 --> 00:02:50,166
So you go,
"What happens to all of Garry's jokes?"

57
00:02:50,570 --> 00:02:51,404
I have them.

58
00:02:51,638 --> 00:02:55,842
This is all of his, like, set lists
and joke journals, and his Rolodex.

59
00:02:56,033 --> 00:02:58,400
Ah! Garry's service, he had a service.

60
00:02:58,500 --> 00:03:00,733
Back then, you'd call your service,
"Do I have any calls?"

61
00:03:00,833 --> 00:03:02,433
-Yeah.
-[both laughing]

62
00:03:02,500 --> 00:03:03,466
"Any calls for me?"

63
00:03:04,333 --> 00:03:06,233
[Judd] And I have a funny one of Jay.

64
00:03:06,299 --> 00:03:07,700
-[Jerry] You're 16 there?
-[Judd] Yeah.

65
00:03:07,766 --> 00:03:09,633
Jay said it looked like
To Catch a Predator.

66
00:03:09,700 --> 00:03:11,566
[both laughing]

67
00:03:11,633 --> 00:03:14,233
You know, I interviewed you
twice back then.

68
00:03:14,333 --> 00:03:16,066
I did 50 of those interviews, 

69
00:03:16,133 --> 00:03:18,466
from Jay, and from Harold Ramis,
and John Candy,

70
00:03:18,566 --> 00:03:20,066
and I just grilled everybody.

71
00:03:20,133 --> 00:03:21,899
I didn't even air
almost any of the interviews.

72
00:03:21,966 --> 00:03:24,100
They were just for me.
I knew no one cared.

73
00:03:24,166 --> 00:03:26,033
And it really was like a textbook,

74
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:29,799
and when I went to college, I thought,
"I don't need to know any of this."

75
00:03:29,866 --> 00:03:33,200
Because people like you explained
everything to me in great detail.

76
00:03:33,266 --> 00:03:36,533
And it really... That's how I felt.
I left college after a year and a half.

77
00:03:36,600 --> 00:03:39,433
You know what I found that's so funny?
I was going through my hoarding.

78
00:03:39,652 --> 00:03:42,055
-This is when I interviewed you...
-[Jerry gasps]

79
00:03:42,133 --> 00:03:44,466
[Judd] ...in 1983.

80
00:03:44,724 --> 00:03:46,559
[Jerry] We gotta do the same pose.

81
00:03:46,633 --> 00:03:47,966
[Judd] That's it.

82
00:03:49,100 --> 00:03:50,899
-[Jerry] Let's get rolling.
-Let's get a hamburger.

83
00:03:50,966 --> 00:03:52,466
[upbeat rock music playing]

84
00:03:53,899 --> 00:03:57,000
Things like this, that are sexy,
make me uncomfortable.

85
00:03:57,066 --> 00:04:00,166
-Because I don't feel sexy.
-Of course you don't, you're not.

86
00:04:00,233 --> 00:04:02,399
But that's where the car takes over.

87
00:04:02,542 --> 00:04:05,878
I have no interest
in good-looking people in movies.

88
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:08,333
-No.
-I would like Bourne Identity much better

89
00:04:08,433 --> 00:04:10,100
-if it starred George Wendt.
-laughs]

90
00:04:10,166 --> 00:04:12,366
I had no car. My dad didn't buy me a car.

91
00:04:12,433 --> 00:04:14,966
Of course you had no car.
It's written all over your face.

92
00:04:15,255 --> 00:04:16,556
That's my car.

93
00:04:16,723 --> 00:04:18,625
-[Jerry] That's fine.
-Does not let me down.

94
00:04:18,791 --> 00:04:19,993
It doesn't lift you up, either.

95
00:04:20,066 --> 00:04:22,300
[engine starts, revs]

96
00:04:22,766 --> 00:04:24,633
-Nice.
-You're in Syosset.

97
00:04:24,700 --> 00:04:26,633
-I'm in Syosset.
-And you get into this.

98
00:04:26,700 --> 00:04:28,599
Mom, Dad,
"When are you coming back, Judd?"

99
00:04:28,666 --> 00:04:30,466
-"I have no idea."
-Yeah. [laughs]

100
00:04:34,866 --> 00:04:37,300
[Judd] I remember I was just starting out,

101
00:04:37,366 --> 00:04:41,000
and I did this bit I used to do
which was a letter to my starving child

102
00:04:41,066 --> 00:04:42,300
in a South American country.

103
00:04:42,448 --> 00:04:44,917
And I slipped it in
right before bringing you on,

104
00:04:44,984 --> 00:04:47,186
-how an emcee does, try to get a joke in.
-Yeah, yeah.

105
00:04:47,266 --> 00:04:50,166
And you walked on stage,
and you went, "That was funny."

106
00:04:50,933 --> 00:04:52,766
And it kept me going for about two years.

107
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:56,900
[upbeat music playing]

108
00:04:56,966 --> 00:04:59,000
What do you want from life at this point?

109
00:04:59,232 --> 00:05:01,100
-Me?
-Yeah. Well, who else?

110
00:05:01,166 --> 00:05:02,133
[laughing]

111
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:04,266
What kind of...
Am I talking to the dashboard?

112
00:05:04,333 --> 00:05:06,400
-You've achieved so much.
-Yeah.

113
00:05:06,466 --> 00:05:09,000
Dreams you never even had. So now...

114
00:05:09,066 --> 00:05:10,133
-Now what?
-Now what?

115
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,766
Well, you know, I started doing
stand-up again two years ago,

116
00:05:13,012 --> 00:05:15,415
and it's very difficult and really fun

117
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,500
to do something
that you've always wanted to do,

118
00:05:17,566 --> 00:05:18,433
so in a way,

119
00:05:18,500 --> 00:05:21,533
I feel like I made all those movies
just to get good spots at The Improv.

120
00:05:21,599 --> 00:05:23,066
-That's hilarious.
-[Judd laughs]

121
00:05:25,800 --> 00:05:27,900
So, you did
The Tonight Show with Jimmy Fallon.

122
00:05:27,966 --> 00:05:29,566
-Yes.
-It was a terrific set.

123
00:05:29,633 --> 00:05:33,433
Any accurate drawing of a Jewish person
is inherently anti-Semitic.

124
00:05:33,533 --> 00:05:34,633
[audience laughing]

125
00:05:34,700 --> 00:05:37,800
A perfect drawing of me
is like Nazi propaganda.

126
00:05:37,866 --> 00:05:39,433
[audience laughing loudly]

127
00:05:39,500 --> 00:05:41,066
That really was the only dream.

128
00:05:41,133 --> 00:05:42,666
-To do The Tonight Show.
-The Tonight Show.

129
00:05:42,909 --> 00:05:45,745
[Judd] Right before I did it,
Louis C.K. sent me an email,

130
00:05:45,833 --> 00:05:49,400
and it explained exactly
how to do The Tonight Show.

131
00:05:49,466 --> 00:05:51,200
With specific directions.

132
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:53,133
Like, "When you walk out to the music,

133
00:05:53,200 --> 00:05:56,300
don't bob your head,
the music's not for you."

134
00:06:00,366 --> 00:06:03,733
I am so excited
about what we're going to eat.

135
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,099
Who decided that
a hamburger's not a breakfast food?

136
00:06:08,266 --> 00:06:09,133
Humans.

137
00:06:09,168 --> 00:06:10,269
[inaudible]

138
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:15,633
[Judd] So, my mom,
she was having financial problems

139
00:06:15,766 --> 00:06:17,166
and she needed to buy a car.

140
00:06:17,233 --> 00:06:19,400
And she got a Mercedes.

141
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:21,866
And I said, "Mom,
why didn't you buy a Camry

142
00:06:21,933 --> 00:06:24,733
so that you had money
to spend on things like food?"

143
00:06:25,233 --> 00:06:27,066
And she said, "Because I'm not an animal."

144
00:06:27,599 --> 00:06:29,366
[Jerry laughing]

145
00:06:31,033 --> 00:06:33,133
[upbeat music playing]

146
00:06:33,200 --> 00:06:37,233
It's fun when you first show a movie
and the audience likes it, the first time.

147
00:06:37,299 --> 00:06:38,866
But then it's kind of gone.

148
00:06:39,098 --> 00:06:40,299
A movie just drifts off,

149
00:06:40,366 --> 00:06:42,435
and at some point,
you feel like you didn't make it.

150
00:06:42,533 --> 00:06:46,133
It's not like stand-up where you can
experience it every night with the crowd,

151
00:06:46,200 --> 00:06:48,900
and you have this
visceral human experience.

152
00:06:49,142 --> 00:06:50,710
The funny thing about making a movie

153
00:06:50,833 --> 00:06:54,533
is you have to convince people
that it will make money.

154
00:06:54,981 --> 00:06:56,549
-Right.
-But you don't know if it will.

155
00:06:57,000 --> 00:06:59,799
When a movie bombs,
you feel pain for a while.

156
00:06:59,866 --> 00:07:01,200
I always say if you make

157
00:07:01,266 --> 00:07:03,133
three mega bombs,
you're out of the business.

158
00:07:03,289 --> 00:07:05,958
If you have a huge bomb,
they'll let you do another.

159
00:07:06,033 --> 00:07:08,033
If that bombs,
they'll let you do a cheaper one,

160
00:07:08,099 --> 00:07:09,500
and if that bombs, you're done.

161
00:07:13,799 --> 00:07:16,166
[Jerry] The idea of this show
is we have a good time.

162
00:07:16,233 --> 00:07:18,666
I've tried to remove
all the annoyances of a talk show.

163
00:07:18,733 --> 00:07:19,833
-Yes.
-The first being,

164
00:07:19,900 --> 00:07:21,433
I don't really want
to talk to this person.

165
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:23,299
[both laughing]

166
00:07:24,166 --> 00:07:25,866
-So, all guests you like?
-Yeah.

167
00:07:25,933 --> 00:07:28,266
[upbeat music playing]

168
00:07:28,666 --> 00:07:31,233
[inaudible]

169
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:36,299
Isn't this great? How funny is this guy?

170
00:07:36,366 --> 00:07:37,333
[Judd] Harvey Korman,

171
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:38,833
-maybe the funniest of all time.
-[Jerry] Yeah.

172
00:07:38,900 --> 00:07:39,766
[inaudible]

173
00:07:44,433 --> 00:07:47,566
Oh, look at Leno. I always hate when
they trick you into doing the toilet shot.

174
00:07:47,633 --> 00:07:49,966
Never do the toilet shot.

175
00:07:50,033 --> 00:07:51,266
Look at the Carson shot!

176
00:07:51,366 --> 00:07:53,166
Look at the look on his face, though.

177
00:07:53,233 --> 00:07:56,033
You're entertained
just by the look in his eye.

178
00:07:56,075 --> 00:07:58,945
That's what I thought was funny,
is Shandling seemed like

179
00:07:59,045 --> 00:08:02,148
they were like buddies from moment one,
like they were comfortable.

180
00:08:02,266 --> 00:08:04,666
-Garry was very, very poised.
-Yeah.

181
00:08:04,733 --> 00:08:06,766
-I envied Garry's poise.
-Yeah.

182
00:08:06,833 --> 00:08:07,766
I didn't have that.

183
00:08:10,289 --> 00:08:12,792
Oh, did you ever just feel like
you landed in heaven?

184
00:08:12,866 --> 00:08:14,233
-Yeah.
-We're in heaven.

185
00:08:14,299 --> 00:08:19,166
Have you ever seen a table that wants
hamburgers and chocolate shakes on it?

186
00:08:19,233 --> 00:08:20,433
-No, this is it.
-This is it.

187
00:08:20,500 --> 00:08:21,700
[waiter] Wanna start with coffee?

188
00:08:21,766 --> 00:08:23,900
-[Jerry] Yeah.
-And a chocolate milkshake.

189
00:08:24,799 --> 00:08:25,633
[whirring]

190
00:08:27,866 --> 00:08:30,166
People don't generally recognize me.

191
00:08:30,333 --> 00:08:34,633
But if I walk down the street, and
somebody says, "Are you Judd Apatow?"

192
00:08:34,900 --> 00:08:37,000
if I say no, they go, "All right."

193
00:08:37,066 --> 00:08:38,566
[both laughing]

194
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:47,400
One of the reasons why I stopped
doing stand-up and performing

195
00:08:47,500 --> 00:08:50,666
is that Adam Sandler and I
auditioned for Jim Henson.

196
00:08:51,133 --> 00:08:52,533
And afterwards, I heard,

197
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:54,500
"Jim Henson doesn't want
to give you the job.

198
00:08:54,566 --> 00:08:56,533
He thought you lacked warmth."

199
00:08:56,600 --> 00:08:59,166
It's like Hitler telling you
you don't have the killer instinct.

200
00:09:02,766 --> 00:09:06,166
Now, when you stopped doing stand-up,
how many year break, 22 years?

201
00:09:06,233 --> 00:09:07,966
I took a 22-year break,

202
00:09:08,033 --> 00:09:10,966
but I hated not being
part of the community.

203
00:09:11,033 --> 00:09:12,500
This never happens.

204
00:09:12,566 --> 00:09:14,233
-This?
-This never happens.

205
00:09:14,300 --> 00:09:17,133
You're not with other comedians,
the whole social--

206
00:09:17,233 --> 00:09:19,433
But if you have a comedian in the movie?

207
00:09:19,492 --> 00:09:22,028
But then you shoot for three months,
and then you work on it

208
00:09:22,094 --> 00:09:23,763
-for a year and a half, alone.
-Oh, I see.

209
00:09:27,466 --> 00:09:30,133
I was so happy with how
Freaks and Geeks came out

210
00:09:30,336 --> 00:09:33,906
that in my head, I thought,
"My career is basically over.

211
00:09:34,240 --> 00:09:36,442
I accomplished
what I wanted to accomplish,

212
00:09:36,533 --> 00:09:39,500
and everything else is gravy."

213
00:09:39,566 --> 00:09:41,566
-Wow.
-And so nothing else matters.

214
00:09:41,933 --> 00:09:44,133
I can experiment, I can do anything,

215
00:09:44,199 --> 00:09:47,699
because I did what I wanted
to do perfectly, once.

216
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,722
That is very healthy.

217
00:09:49,800 --> 00:09:52,033
I don't think
you need to go to the shrink anymore.

218
00:09:52,100 --> 00:09:53,366
I think you're right.

219
00:09:56,433 --> 00:09:57,933
Are you anti-therapy?

220
00:09:58,333 --> 00:09:59,166
Kind of, yeah.

221
00:09:59,233 --> 00:10:01,100
Because it seems self-perpetuating.

222
00:10:01,166 --> 00:10:02,166
I could talk with anybody.

223
00:10:02,234 --> 00:10:04,770
You could say, "What's bothering you?"
I'll go for an hour.

224
00:10:04,866 --> 00:10:05,800
[both laugh]

225
00:10:05,866 --> 00:10:07,800
[soft jazz music playing]

226
00:10:11,199 --> 00:10:12,500
[Judd] When Garry died,

227
00:10:12,566 --> 00:10:18,033
they decided not to do an autopsy,
because what was really the point?

228
00:10:18,217 --> 00:10:20,219
And then I see Albert Brooks,
and he's like,

229
00:10:20,300 --> 00:10:21,666
"Was there an autopsy?"

230
00:10:21,733 --> 00:10:22,566
[Jerry laughs]

231
00:10:22,633 --> 00:10:25,066
I'm like, "No,
they decided there was no reason."

232
00:10:25,266 --> 00:10:27,100
And he's like, "Well, there is a reason,

233
00:10:27,199 --> 00:10:29,699
so I could know what happened
and try to avoid it."

234
00:10:29,766 --> 00:10:31,366
[chuckling]

235
00:10:32,166 --> 00:10:35,066
At the memorial, everybody who spoke,

236
00:10:35,133 --> 00:10:38,433
all said things
that Garry had said to them,

237
00:10:38,900 --> 00:10:41,600
and every person who spoke killed with it.

238
00:10:41,699 --> 00:10:46,300
Garry's lawyer rips the roof
off the place quoting Garry.

239
00:10:46,366 --> 00:10:48,533
[both laughing]

240
00:10:49,366 --> 00:10:53,466
You know, people responded
at Garry's memorial to you and Garry.

241
00:10:53,533 --> 00:10:55,266
-And it's a big way.
-That's nice.

242
00:10:55,733 --> 00:10:58,833
There's something about him
that encompasses the human struggle.

243
00:10:59,233 --> 00:11:02,133
Like, he was trying so hard to be happy,

244
00:11:02,199 --> 00:11:04,766
and to find peace,
and to let go of his ego.

245
00:11:05,966 --> 00:11:07,100
You're making me sad.

246
00:11:11,733 --> 00:11:13,433
And he touched people very deeply.

247
00:11:13,500 --> 00:11:15,600
And Garry always said,
"The Larry Sanders Show

248
00:11:15,808 --> 00:11:19,478
is about people who love each other,
but show business gets in the way."

249
00:11:23,366 --> 00:11:25,666
I need that cheeseburger so bad right now.

250
00:11:25,733 --> 00:11:27,866
How hard is it to make a cheeseburger?

251
00:11:28,266 --> 00:11:30,066
It's a diner. Where is it?

252
00:11:33,633 --> 00:11:34,733
[bell rings]

253
00:11:34,800 --> 00:11:36,800
[Judd] Here's something I do
when it comes to food.

254
00:11:36,866 --> 00:11:39,966
I always find a rationalization
why it's okay.

255
00:11:40,033 --> 00:11:41,533
Really? You're the one that does that?

256
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:43,000
[both laughing]

257
00:11:43,100 --> 00:11:45,433
Do you find there's anything with money

258
00:11:45,500 --> 00:11:47,600
that makes you happy
more than simple things?

259
00:11:47,666 --> 00:11:50,066
-Oh, yeah.
-[both laughing]

260
00:11:50,133 --> 00:11:51,800
I never have anything I want to buy.

261
00:11:51,900 --> 00:11:54,699
I have no item that I like
that costs anything.

262
00:11:55,166 --> 00:11:57,500
-You have no taste.
-[laughing]

263
00:12:02,466 --> 00:12:05,199
Both my kids are really funny,
and my daughter, who's 13,

264
00:12:05,391 --> 00:12:08,461
the other night, she says,
"I don't want you and Mom to have sex."

265
00:12:09,633 --> 00:12:11,433
And I go, "What are you talking about?"

266
00:12:11,500 --> 00:12:13,133
And she goes, "Six months ago,

267
00:12:13,365 --> 00:12:15,735
I walked up to the door
and I heard you have sex,

268
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,166
and it really freaked me out."

269
00:12:17,233 --> 00:12:19,566
Maybe you were just trying
to get a plug in an outlet.

270
00:12:19,633 --> 00:12:21,400
[laughing]

271
00:12:21,566 --> 00:12:23,600
I think we were watching, like, Narcos.

272
00:12:24,100 --> 00:12:27,033
-I think she heard Escobar have sex.
-[laughs] Yeah, right.

273
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:32,866
Actually, an interesting marker is,

274
00:12:32,966 --> 00:12:36,400
at what age kids are allowed
to have tattoos without permission.

275
00:12:36,466 --> 00:12:38,666
Never. I just don't like the idea of it.

276
00:12:38,733 --> 00:12:40,733
Draw on a piece of paper
if you want to draw.

277
00:12:44,266 --> 00:12:45,833
I could not make that any better.

278
00:12:46,400 --> 00:12:49,133
I feel like I don't want
to know where this place is,

279
00:12:49,199 --> 00:12:50,900
because I might just go back all the time.

280
00:12:50,936 --> 00:12:53,973
It's like when I was a very young man,
I did mushrooms,

281
00:12:54,099 --> 00:12:58,033
and I went to see Frank Sinatra
and Don Rickles on mushrooms.

282
00:12:58,099 --> 00:13:00,233
And, my friend, on mushrooms,

283
00:13:00,500 --> 00:13:04,400
was brought on stage by Rickles,
and we had the best night of our lives.

284
00:13:04,450 --> 00:13:06,819
We talked about it for years.
We'd call each other and go,

285
00:13:06,900 --> 00:13:08,300
"Best night of our lives, right?"

286
00:13:08,366 --> 00:13:09,633
[Jerry laughing]

287
00:13:09,800 --> 00:13:12,300
Afterwards, I said to my friend,
"Can I get more mushrooms?"

288
00:13:12,366 --> 00:13:15,266
And he said,
"Oh, I lost the contact of the guy."

289
00:13:15,800 --> 00:13:17,133
Because he could see in my eyes

290
00:13:17,363 --> 00:13:20,599
I was about to do mushrooms
every day for the rest of my life.

291
00:13:20,666 --> 00:13:23,366
-So, you relate that to this burger?
-Exactly.

292
00:13:23,433 --> 00:13:24,866
Don't tell me where this diner is.

293
00:13:27,333 --> 00:13:30,466
What is it when someone's funny?
Why do we love them so much?

294
00:13:30,733 --> 00:13:35,666
When I was a kid, I loved that people
were saying "all of this makes no sense."

295
00:13:35,733 --> 00:13:40,766
The whole setup of this life is crazy,
and who's rich and who's poor,

296
00:13:40,833 --> 00:13:41,933
and who has power,

297
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:44,166
and the emotional life of people,
the spirituality,

298
00:13:44,266 --> 00:13:46,500
and I love that comedians decoded it all.

299
00:13:46,566 --> 00:13:48,699
I love that comedians do that.

300
00:13:48,833 --> 00:13:50,766
I don't think I could survive without it.

301
00:13:52,333 --> 00:13:55,233
[upbeat music playing]

302
00:13:57,966 --> 00:14:00,966
[inaudible]

303
00:14:01,033 --> 00:14:03,699
[Judd] When I was a kid,
my parents got divorced,

304
00:14:03,766 --> 00:14:06,800
and my mother got a job seating people

305
00:14:06,866 --> 00:14:08,400
at the East End Comedy Club...

306
00:14:08,466 --> 00:14:09,800
-What?
-...in Southampton.

307
00:14:10,115 --> 00:14:12,818
I could not have loved it more.

308
00:14:12,900 --> 00:14:16,000
I watched every single show
for an entire summer.

309
00:14:16,066 --> 00:14:17,166
I always thought

310
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:22,033
my mom couldn't have gotten paid
any money to seat people at a comedy club.

311
00:14:22,099 --> 00:14:22,933
No.

312
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,966
What do you pay a woman
to seat people, 50 bucks?

313
00:14:26,165 --> 00:14:28,968
And how embarrassing is it
as an upper middle class woman...

314
00:14:29,033 --> 00:14:30,599
-Right.
-...who has a horrible divorce.

315
00:14:30,666 --> 00:14:32,533
And she knew I loved comedy

316
00:14:33,300 --> 00:14:38,000
And I think she must have, on some level,
thought, "Judd would love this."

317
00:14:38,900 --> 00:14:40,133
That's sweet.

318
00:14:43,133 --> 00:14:45,033
And I remember Lenny Schultz came in.

319
00:14:45,400 --> 00:14:47,533
-And Lenny Schultz had that crazy act.
-Mmm-hmm.

320
00:14:47,599 --> 00:14:51,000
And how it ended was
they put plastic down on the stage,

321
00:14:51,066 --> 00:14:53,033
he would put on this Italian opera music,

322
00:14:53,158 --> 00:14:54,760
he'd have a big thing of spaghetti,

323
00:14:54,827 --> 00:14:57,997
and he would lip-sync
and throw the spaghetti in his face.

324
00:14:58,500 --> 00:15:01,633
It ended, like, pouring milk over his head
and cracking eggs on his head.

325
00:15:01,699 --> 00:15:03,500
-Yeah.
-And then he would come in the kitchen,

326
00:15:03,566 --> 00:15:05,066
and jump in the giant sink,

327
00:15:05,166 --> 00:15:08,266
and I would have to pour 
buckets of water over his head

328
00:15:08,440 --> 00:15:10,976
to get all the spaghetti and eggs
off of him.

329
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:12,711
And I could not have been happier.

330
00:15:16,382 --> 00:15:17,850
I think we got that done.

331
00:15:18,117 --> 00:15:20,352
-Fantastic.
-I think there's 17 minutes in there.

332
00:15:20,433 --> 00:15:21,366
[Jerry] No question.

333
00:15:21,433 --> 00:15:22,766
[both laughing]

334
00:15:30,199 --> 00:15:31,966
Well, I don't necessarily believe in God.

335
00:15:32,033 --> 00:15:34,900
Like, the only way I could process
being a human being

336
00:15:35,199 --> 00:15:37,666
is to examine it, and learn,

337
00:15:37,733 --> 00:15:41,633
and also to mock everything
and to see the ridiculousness of it,

338
00:15:41,674 --> 00:15:43,575
because there's no order in my mind.

339
00:15:43,642 --> 00:15:45,377
I don't even know
what you're talking about.

340
00:15:45,444 --> 00:15:47,246
[Judd laughing]

